That is the confirmation I wanted, and it makes the case cleanly: a field c=
an keep its place in the file and still stop meaning what it meant, and the=
 loader has no way to tell that apart from a checkpoint pointing at the wro=
ng log.

On your question, I would leave the older files exactly as they are. Rewrit=
ing a checkpoint so it can satisfy a check we are in the middle of relaxing=
 is work we would throw away, and it would also destroy the one honest reco=
rd of what that older build counted. The comparison instead looks at the fi=
elds that tie the checkpoint to its log =E2=80=94 the response count and th=
e author on the last entry =E2=80=94 and those it must match exactly, since=
 a disagreement there means the file describes something else and we should=
 refuse it loudly. Everything else in the sidecar stays in the file and sta=
ys in the dump, because it is worth reading when a run goes wrong. It simpl=
y stops having a vote on whether the checkpoint loads.

